# The Epic of Gilgamesh, related Mesopotamian myths and records; no villains or monsters, other than Ea-nasir.
gilgamesh    king of Uruk, the hero of the epic
enkidu       the wild man who becomes Gilgamesh's friend
shamhat      priestess who brings Enkidu to the city
ninsun       goddess, Gilgamesh's mother, interpreter of dreams
lugalbanda   king of Uruk, Gilgamesh's father
aruru        goddess who shapes Enkidu from clay
siduri       alewife at the edge of the world who counsels Gilgamesh
urshanabi    ferryman across the Waters of Death
utnapishtim  survivor of the flood, granted immortality
shamash      sun god, Gilgamesh's protector
ishtar       goddess of love and war
anu          god of the sky
enlil        god of the air, head of the pantheon
adad         storm god who brings the flood
inanna       Sumerian goddess, Inanna's Descent to the Netherworld
ninshubur    Inanna's loyal minister, who rescues her from the netherworld
dumuzi       shepherd, Inanna's consort
geshtinanna  Dumuzi's sister, who shares his time in the netherworld
ereshkigal   queen of the netherworld
ningishzida  guardian god of the netherworld and of vegetation
enki         god of wisdom and fresh water (Ea)
nammu        goddess of the primeval sea, Enki's mother
ninhursag    mother goddess of the mountains
atrahasis    flood hero of the Atrahasis epic
ziusudra     flood hero of the Sumerian version
adapa        sage of Eridu who refuses the bread of life
apkallu      the seven sages who taught humans the arts
etana        king who rises to heaven on an eagle
enmerkar     king of Uruk, credited with inventing writing
marduk       god of Babylon, Enuma Elish
nabu         god of writing and wisdom
nisaba       goddess of writing and grain, patron of scribes
ninurta      warrior god, Lugal-e
nanna        god of the moon
ningal       goddess, Nanna's consort
utu          sun god of the Sumerian version (Shamash)
ninkasi      goddess of beer, Hymn to Ninkasi
lamassu      protective spirit guarding the city gates
nanni        customer of Ur whose tablet to Ea-nasir is the oldest known written complaint
ea-nasir     copper merchant of Ur, famous for selling poor copper
